": "https://doi.org/10.51952/9781529237191.ch013", "pdf_url": "https://bristoluniversitypressdigital.com/downloadpdf/edcollchap-oa/book/9781529237191/ch013.pdf", "source": {"id": "https://openalex.org/S4306462973", "display_name": "Bristol University Press eBooks", "issn_l": null, "issn": null, "is_oa": false, "is_in_doaj": false, "is_core": false, "listed_in": [], "host_organization": null, "host_organization_name": null, "host_organization_lineage": [], "host_organization_lineage_names": [], "type": "ebook platform"}, "license": "cc-by-nc-nd", "license_id": "https://openalex.org/licenses/cc-by-nc-nd", "version": "publishedVersion", "is_accepted": true, "is_published": true, "raw_source_name": "The Realities of Autonomous Weapons", "raw_type": "book-chapter"}, "abstract": "Over a decade’s worth of discussions on the ethical and legal implications of autonomous weapon systems have yielded limited results. Complicating matters is the fact that, to date, the debate is marred by unhelpful conflations, imprecision of terms and a lack of agreement about the meaning of key terms. It begins with the term ‘autonomy’ and stretches to concepts such as trust and responsibility. This is perhaps not surprising given the speed with which the technology develops and the fact that the debate is transdisciplinary in nature. Trust, for example, has very different layers of meaning in an engineering context than it has from the perspective of a social scientist, psychologist or philosopher. Increasingly, however, the forms and imaginaries framing discussions on moral agency and responsibility take on the markers of technical discourse, even if they are held within a philosophical register. In such discussions, both human agency and machine agency are read through a technological lens wherein functional equivalences are drawn between the two in order to make one fit the other. In this chapter, I examine these discourses, their logical foundations and associated imaginaries, and argue that rather than help make sense of the specific demand of moral agency and responsibility in the context of lethal autonomous weapon systems (LAWS), this takes us further away from understanding moral concerns as distinctly human concerns, and further into the terrain of thinking about ethics as a purely technological problem that can be solved with more attentiveness to technology rather than to human relations. This creates critical blindspots in the debate on moral responsibility for LAWS."}]", "excerpt_truncated": true, "source_sha256": "b09d7e790ad4b9ae2de66b260998c69b92d02a8da9fa11126ef1441ca43c2e0c", "verification_required": true, "topic_domain": "responsibility_formation", "evidence_role": "discovery", "host_tier": "discovery", "source_identity": null, "persistent_identifiers": ["doi:10.60087/jaigs.v8i1.363", "doi:10.51952/9781529237191.ch013", "openalex:W4410431452"], "source_candidates": ["https://doi.org/10.60087/jaigs.v8i1.363", "https://doi.org/10.51952/9781529237191.ch013"], "source_candidate_identities": {}}

